(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["cam-nutrition-exam-1-special-topics-nutrition-quiz"]={"title":"Special Topics in Nutrition — CAM/Nutrition Exam 1","category":"CAM / Nutrition","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which best matches the lecture's definition of malnutrition?","choices":["A permanent and completely irreversible loss of lean body mass that occurs regardless of nutrition support, medical treatment, or underlying disease process.","A single micronutrient or vitamin deficiency identified incidentally on routine bloodwork, unrelated to body composition or function.","Any diagnosis of obesity in a hospitalized patient, regardless of their muscle mass, intake adequacy, or inflammatory status.","An acute, subacute, or chronic nutrition state where over- or under-nutrition, with or without inflammation, leads to altered body composition and diminished function."],"answer":3,"exp":"Malnutrition is defined broadly as a nutrition state (acute, subacute, or chronic) combining varying degrees of over- or under-nutrition, with or without inflammation, that changes body composition and diminishes function. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What are the three etiology-based nutrition diagnoses used in adults in clinical practice?","choices":["Type 1 malnutrition, Type 2 malnutrition, and Type 3 malnutrition classifications","Community-acquired malnutrition, hospital-acquired malnutrition, and iatrogenic malnutrition","Starvation-related, chronic disease-related, and acute disease/injury-related malnutrition","Mild malnutrition, moderate malnutrition, and severe malnutrition severity grades"],"answer":2,"exp":"The three etiology-based categories are starvation-related, chronic disease-related, and acute disease- or injury-related malnutrition. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What distinguishes simple starvation from stress starvation?","choices":["Simple starvation is defined as a condition that only affects pediatric patients; stress starvation is defined as a condition that only affects elderly adults with multiple comorbidities.","Simple starvation is characterized by dramatic electrolyte shifts into cells; stress starvation is characterized by the complete absence of any electrolyte changes.","Simple starvation occurs when a healthy individual stops eating; stress starvation occurs when a starved individual is also subject to the metabolic response of trauma, sepsis, or critical illness.","Simple starvation is always fatal within days regardless of treatment; stress starvation is always fully reversible once feeding resumes."],"answer":2,"exp":"Simple starvation is a healthy individual simply not eating; stress starvation adds the metabolic response of trauma, sepsis, or critical illness on top of inadequate intake. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Per the prevalence data cited in lecture, what percentage of hospitalized US adults are estimated to be malnourished?","choices":["70% to 90%","1% to 5%","Nearly 100%","20% to 50%"],"answer":3,"exp":"20% to 50% of hospitalized US adults are estimated to be malnourished. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Per lecture, how much longer is hospital length of stay for malnourished older adults compared to well-nourished older adults?","choices":["10% to 20% longer","Length of stay is unaffected by malnutrition status.","40% to 70% longer","5% to 10% longer"],"answer":2,"exp":"Length of stay is 40% to 70% longer in older adults who are malnourished. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"According to lecture, what degree of lean body mass loss is associated with impaired wound healing?","choices":["15-20% loss","Loss of lean body mass does not affect wound healing.","5% loss","10% loss"],"answer":0,"exp":"A 15-20% loss of lean body mass will impair wound healing. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"According to lecture, a 10% loss of lean body mass is most closely associated with which consequence?","choices":["Immune suppression and increased risk of infection","Delayed and impaired wound healing capacity","Progressively impaired cardiac contractile function","Development of spontaneous pressure ulcers over bony prominences"],"answer":0,"exp":"A 10% loss of lean body mass causes immune suppression and increases infection risk. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed as a malnutrition risk factor that develops AFTER hospital admission, rather than before it?","choices":["Long-term nursing home placement","Longstanding preexisting chronic illness","Chronic household food insecurity","NPO/fasting status for procedures"],"answer":3,"exp":"NPO/fasting for procedures is listed among the \"after admit\" risk factors, along with acute illness, worsening chronic illness, need for mealtime assistance, and meal restrictions. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"When should a nutrition screen be completed on a hospitalized patient, per lecture?","choices":["Only at discharge","Within the first week of admission","Within 24 hours of admission","Only if the patient reports unintentional weight loss"],"answer":2,"exp":"The nutrition screen should be completed within 24 hours of admission, typically as part of nursing admission. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A patient's usual body weight (UBW) was 210 lbs and current weight is 170 lbs. What is the percent weight loss?","choices":["About 40%","About 24%","About 10%","About 19%"],"answer":3,"exp":"(210-170)/210 = 40/210 ≈ 19% weight loss, matching the lecture's own worked example. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Per ASPEN significant-weight-loss guidelines cited in lecture, which pairing of timeframe and percent loss is correct?","choices":["1 week: 2%","30 days: 2%","6 months: 5%","1 week: 10%"],"answer":0,"exp":"ASPEN's significant-weight-loss guideline pairs 1 week with 2% loss (also: 30 days with 5%, 3 months with 7.5%, and 6 months with 10%). — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What BMI range is described in lecture as usually incompatible with life?","choices":["18.5–20","20–22","11–13","15–17"],"answer":2,"exp":"A BMI of 11–13 is usually incompatible with life, per lecture. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Why doesn't a normal BMI rule out malnutrition, per lecture?","choices":["BMI is never actually measured or documented for malnourished patients admitted to the hospital.","Many malnourished patients have normal or above-normal BMIs due to residual obesity or an expanded extracellular fluid volume.","Malnutrition invariably causes BMI to rise well above the normal reference range in every patient.","BMI calculations are only considered clinically valid for pediatric and adolescent patient populations."],"answer":1,"exp":"Many patients with malnutrition have normal or above-normal BMIs because of residual obesity or expanded extracellular fluid volume (e.g. edema), so BMI alone can mask malnutrition. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Per lecture, how many of the 6 ASPEN characteristics need to be present to diagnose malnutrition?","choices":["1 of 6","All 6","2 of 6","4 of 6"],"answer":2,"exp":"2 of the 6 ASPEN characteristics should be present to diagnose malnutrition. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which malnutrition-related lab marker has the shortest half-life, per lecture?","choices":["Transferrin, with a half-life of about 8 to 10 days","Prealbumin, with a half-life of about 3 days","Albumin, with a half-life of about 3 weeks","Retinol binding protein (12 hours)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Retinol binding protein has the shortest half-life of the four markers listed, at about 12 hours. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Why can't albumin alone reliably diagnose malnutrition, per lecture?","choices":["Albumin levels remain essentially identical across every disease state, regardless of inflammation, hydration, or organ dysfunction.","Albumin simply cannot be measured with any reliability in hospitalized or critically ill patient populations at any point.","Albumin levels only reflect dietary protein intake from approximately the past 12 hours, similar to prealbumin.","Hypoalbuminemia won't improve with nutrition therapy alone as long as systemic inflammation persists, and it's affected by hydration status and organ disease."],"answer":3,"exp":"Albumin is affected by inflammation, stress, renal/liver disease, hydration status, and certain medications — hypoalbuminemia won't improve with nutrition support alone if systemic inflammation persists. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What is enteral nutrition?","choices":["Any diet consisting only of liquid nutrition supplements taken by mouth rather than by tube.","A structured diet restricted to fewer than 800 kilocalories consumed per day.","Nutrition delivered directly into the bloodstream intravenously.","Nutrition fed into the GI tract using a special tube, bypassing the mouth."],"answer":3,"exp":"Enteral nutrition is nutrition fed into the GI tract via a special tube, bypassing the mouth. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What is parenteral nutrition?","choices":["Any diet supervised by a registered dietitian.","Nutrition fed into the GI tract via a tube.","Nutrition delivered via the vein (IV).","Oral nutrition supplements taken between meals."],"answer":2,"exp":"Parenteral nutrition is IV nutrition, delivered directly into a vein. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What is the guiding clinical principle for choosing enteral over parenteral nutrition, per lecture?","choices":["\"If the gut works, use it\" — enteral nutrition is more physiologic, lower risk, and more cost-effective.","\"Always begin with whichever route is least invasive, regardless of gut function or overall cost.\"","\"Parenteral nutrition should always be attempted first, since it bypasses digestion entirely.\"","\"Route selection should depend only on the patient's insurance coverage and copay structure.\""],"answer":0,"exp":"\"If the gut works, use it\" — enteral nutrition is preferred because it's more physiologic, carries lower complication risk, and is more cost-effective than parenteral nutrition. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which of the following is a contraindication for enteral nutrition (and thus an indication for parenteral nutrition instead)?","choices":["Severe ileus or feeding intolerance","Recent uncomplicated dental work","Mild nausea after meals","A functional GI tract with good appetite"],"answer":0,"exp":"Severe ileus or feeding intolerance is a listed contraindication for enteral nutrition (along with intestinal obstruction, hemodynamic instability, severe malabsorption, major GI bleed, and major GI surgery requiring gut rest). — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is the difference between peripheral (PPN) and total (TPN) parenteral nutrition?","choices":["PPN is restricted to use only in pediatric patients under age five; TPN is restricted to use only in adult patients over sixty-five.","PPN and TPN are simply two different names describing the exact same peripheral-line infusion procedure and nutrient formula.","PPN requires surgically placed central venous access; TPN can be delivered safely through a simple peripheral IV line.","PPN is delivered through a peripheral line for a few days to 2 weeks with limited osmolarity/calories; TPN needs central access but can provide full nutritional needs."],"answer":3,"exp":"PPN uses a peripheral venous line, limited to a few days to 2 weeks with restricted osmolarity and calorie/protein/micronutrient delivery; TPN requires central access but can meet full nutritional needs. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which of the following must be corrected BEFORE starting parenteral nutrition, per lecture's contraindications?","choices":["Mild, self-limited constipation lasting a few days","A low-grade fever below 100.5 degrees Fahrenheit","A recent minor bruise on the forearm from a blood draw","Hyperglycemia, electrolyte abnormalities, and severe fluid overload"],"answer":3,"exp":"Hyperglycemia, electrolyte abnormalities, and severe fluid overload need to be corrected prior to starting parenteral nutrition. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What is refeeding syndrome?","choices":["A specific surgical complication of gastric bypass procedures, caused by anastomotic leakage and localized infection at the staple line.","An IgE-mediated hypersensitivity allergic reaction triggered by protein components in standard enteral tube-feeding formula, causing hives and anaphylaxis.","A completely normal, expected, and harmless part of resuming a full oral diet after any illness, surgery, or hospitalization.","A metabolic complication when nutrition support is given to severely malnourished patients, where a shift from catabolism to anabolism drives electrolytes into cells."],"answer":3,"exp":"Refeeding syndrome is a metabolic complication triggered when nutrition support is administered to severely malnourished patients — the shift from catabolism to anabolism (and dextrose to insulin) drives electrolytes into the cells. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What three electrolyte abnormalities characterize refeeding syndrome?","choices":["Hypophosphatemia, hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia","Hyperphosphatemia, hyperkalemia, hypermagnesemia","Hyponatremia, hypochloremia, hypocalcemia","Hypernatremia, hyperkalemia, hypercalcemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Refeeding syndrome is characterized by hypophosphatemia, hypokalemia, and hypomagnesemia. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What must be given before any dextrose is administered to a refeeding-risk patient, and why?","choices":["Potassium, given preemptively to correct an anticipated episode of hyperkalemia before feeding begins","Vitamin C, given at a high dose to boost immune function before carbohydrate refeeding begins","Thiamine (B1), because the shift to carbohydrate metabolism requires it as a cofactor and stores are often already depleted","Insulin, administered proactively to prevent an anticipated spike in blood glucose during refeeding"],"answer":2,"exp":"Thiamine (B1) must be given before any dextrose — it's a required cofactor for carbohydrate metabolism, and depleted patients often already have low thiamine stores. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What neurological complication can result from untreated thiamine deficiency during refeeding?","choices":["Bell's palsy with unilateral facial nerve paralysis","Wernicke's and Korsakoff encephalopathy","Trigeminal neuralgia with episodic facial pain","Guillain-Barré syndrome with ascending muscle paralysis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Untreated thiamine deficiency during refeeding can lead to Wernicke's and Korsakoff encephalopathy. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What micronutrients matter most for pressure wound healing, per lecture?","choices":["Zinc, vitamin A, and vitamin C","Vitamin B12, folate, and iron","Sodium, potassium, and magnesium","Iron, vitamin D, and calcium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Zinc, vitamin A, and vitamin C are the micronutrients emphasized for pressure wound healing (with a caution not to over-supplement vitamin A, which can cause toxicity). — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"How does dietary protein restriction factor into managing chronic liver disease, per current lecture guidance?","choices":["Protein intake should be significantly restricted in every case of chronic liver disease to directly lower serum ammonia levels.","Protein restriction is no longer recommended — it doesn't improve hepatic encephalopathy and instead breaks down muscle mass, raising ammonia levels.","Protein requirements steadily and predictably decrease as chronic liver disease advances toward end-stage cirrhosis and ascites.","Protein intake should be entirely eliminated from the diet as soon as clinically significant ascites first develops in these patients."],"answer":1,"exp":"Protein restriction is no longer recommended in chronic liver disease — it fails to improve hepatic encephalopathy and instead promotes muscle breakdown, which raises ammonia levels. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"In chronic kidney disease stages 3-5 (pre-dialysis), which nutrients should typically be reduced?","choices":["Carbohydrates and dietary fiber exclusively","Water only, with no other dietary restrictions","Fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, and K","Sodium, phosphorus, potassium, and protein"],"answer":3,"exp":"CKD stages 3-5 (pre-dialysis) call for reduced sodium, phosphorus, potassium, and protein intake. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What nutritional priority applies to trauma patients, per lecture?","choices":["Restrict protein intake substantially during the first week after injury to help minimize the body's overall metabolic stress response and inflammation.","Start nutrition support early — trauma causes a hypermetabolic state with increased calorie and protein needs for wound healing and to prevent lean muscle loss.","Delay nutrition support until the patient is fully hemodynamically stable, often waiting a week or more before starting.","Avoid enteral feeding entirely and rely instead on immediately starting parenteral nutrition delivered through a central line."],"answer":1,"exp":"Trauma patients should start nutrition support early, since trauma produces a hypermetabolic state with increased calorie and high protein needs for wound healing and preventing lean muscle loss. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 48"}]};
